0..50 Comment "//! One list with two memory layouts, after Ribbit"
51..129 Comment "//! [Baudon-2024-algebraic_data_types_highperformance_computing], Example 3.4:"
130..205 Comment "//! the list of `i32` laid out pointer-tagged, one element per pointer, and"
206..284 Comment "//! packed, two elements per pointer with a lone last element inline. A layout"
285..364 Comment "//! is a second data type over the list\'s code, admitted by an equivalence with"
365..439 Comment "//! the list whose round trips are proved by recursion and replayed by the"
440..514 Comment "//! kernel; `ua` makes each equivalence a path between types, so a program"
515..592 Comment "//! written once over the list runs on either layout by transport and a value"
593..664 Comment "//! moves between layouts along their composite. What is checked is the"
665..741 Comment "//! equivalence. The layouts are data, not bytes: each constructor\'s comment"
742..822 Comment "//! names the memory type Ribbit gives it, and code emitted from a layout stands"
823..901 Comment "//! outside the trust boundary. `equiv` decides no round trip over a type that"
902..955 Comment "//! holds itself, so each equivalence is written out."
956..959 Comment "//!"
960..976 Comment "//! - see: #1316"
977..1050 Comment "//! - cites: [Baudon-2024-algebraic_data_types_highperformance_computing]"
1051..1054 Comment "//!"
1055..1244 Comment "//! [Baudon-2024-algebraic_data_types_highperformance_computing]: https://doi.org/10.70675/7bb9b51ezf689z4cb5z8d80z794397b5f052 \"Baudon, Algebraic Data Types for High-Performance Computing\""
